  "image_name": "bdfc5555e9af06774ca4ed40d7e7d077.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several interfacial mechanisms that influence spin behavior in layered magnetic or catalytic materials. Overall, it shows that the surface or interface can strongly modify the bulk through spin coupling, charge redistribution, and exchange pathways.\n\nOne schematic highlights generic interfacial spin interactions between a surface layer and the underlying bulk, implying that spins at the boundary affect the material beneath. Another compares a ferromagnet interfaced with either an antiferromagnetic or paramagnetic layer. In both cases, the neighboring layer appears to impose partial spin alignment at the interface, producing a spin pinning effect that influences the ferromagnetic spins.\n\nA band-diagram style panel illustrates a built-in electric field across an n-type NiFe-LDH region and p-type Co3O4. The bands bend at the junction, indicating charge separation and spin transfer across the interface, with electrons and holes moving in opposite directions under the internal field.\n\nThe final schematic proposes an “interfacial double exchange” mechanism between Co3O4 and NiFeOOH. It depicts spin-polarized transport through metal–oxygen–metal channels involving Co, Ni, and Fe ions, suggesting that oxygen bridges help mediate spin-selective exchange and facilitate interfacial reaction steps associated with oxygen-containing species.",
